{"_data": [{"type": "metadata", "contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": "Issues in the Workplace"}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "What Do You Think?"}, {"type": "position", "content": 5}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 15}, {"type": "level", "content": "B2.2"}]}, {"type": "achievment_test", "contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}]}, {"type": "objectives", "contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": " Describe  necessity, duty, advice, or strong dissaproval in in written and spoken situations according to work and social contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_2", "content": "Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_3", "content": "Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_4", "content": "Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_5", "content": "Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_6", "content": "Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_7", "content": "Understands the main ideas and specific information of complex text on both concrete and abstract topics in written texts in social and work contexts."}, {"type": "secondary_8", "content": "Integrates a wider range of written and spoken language skills and knowledge when dealing with problems and difficulties in the workplace."}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to print out"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to use a computer printer to print something"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I printed out extra copies in case more people showed up."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn10le15ob05aud01"}]}, {"type": "target_word_describer", "contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use to talk about responsibilities at work."}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to sit through"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to \u200bstay until the end of an \u200bevent such as a \u200bmeeting or presentation "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: To be honest, I wasn't expecting a lot of people to show up let alone sit through the presentation. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn10le15ob05aud02"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to ramble on "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to \u200btalk in a \u200bconfused way, often for a \u200blong \u200btime"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: I may have been rambling on for all I know. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn10le15ob05aud03"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to overanalyze"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to examine something in \u200bdetail more than it needs to be in \u200border to \u200bdiscover more about it"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: If you will overanalyze this, you won't get anywhere."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn10le15ob05aud04"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to come across"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to \u200bbehave in a way that makes \u200bpeople \u200bbelieve that you have a \u200bparticular \u200bcharacteristic"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: You might have came across a bit too strong. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn10le15ob05aud05"}]}, {"type": "target_word", "contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "to set out"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to \u200bstart an \u200bactivity with a \u200bparticular \u200baim"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example:I won't have accomplished what I set out to do!"}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn10le15ob05aud06"}]}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "engenn10le15ob05re1img01.jpg"}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "If-Clauses Expressing Strong Disapproval"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "When you talk about conditions, you use if-clauses. You can use these to express different opinions or emotions about what someone else <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">did/does/will do</span></span> or<span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\"> says/said/will say</span></span>."}, {"type": "text", "content": "You sometimes use <span class=\"strong\"><span class=\"italic\">will</span></span> and <span class=\"strong\"><span class=\"italic\">won't</span></span> in the if-clause with the meanings of strong disapproval. When someone insists on doing something, and you disagree with it, you can express that feeling.  </br></br>For example:"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"italic\">If you<span class=\"strong\"> will </span>drive carelessly, you must be expecting to have an accident. </span>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "If you won't calm down, you must accept that I'm not telling you what I thought."}, {"type": "example", "content": "If you will correct them, you not only interrupt their train of thought but it will also affect their confidence."}, {"type": "example", "content": "If you will keep up with this, you must have a chat with Denise."}, {"type": "example", "content": "If you will overanalyze this, you won't get anywhere."}, {"type": "example", "content": "If you will hand out a survey, you must be prepared for both positive and negative feedback."}, {"type": "example", "content": "If you won't put time and effort into this, you must expect to see no changes around the office. "}]}, {"type": "learning_points", "contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Modals in the Past to Speculate the Possibility of Something"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "When you want to talk about the possibility of something happening or not, you can use modals."}, {"type": "text", "content": "The modals for speculating about past possibility are:</br></br>may (not) have </br> might (not) have</br> could (not) have</br> will (not) have"}, {"type": "text", "content": "For example:  </br></br><span class=\"italic\">She <span class=\"strong\">may have </span>already done it, for all we know. </br>The boss <span class=\"strong\">won't have</span> had the chance to look over the report yet. </br>That wasn't a good idea - you <span class=\"strong\">might have</span> caused more trouble! </br> You <span class=\"strong\">could have </span>done just enough to finish."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Notice you use the past participle after each of these modal phrases.  </br></br> Look at the examples from the text:"}, {"type": "example", "content": "I may have been rambling on for all I know. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "You might have came across a bit too strong. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "I could have included a few more here and there now that I think about it. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "I won't have accomplished what I set out to do!"}]}, {"type": "takeaway_assessment", "contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "I discuss issues related to work."}, {"type": "text", "content